I’m going to describe a Snowflake project. Produce a numbered step-by-step build plan where each step has a short prompt I will paste into Cortex Code, Snowflake’s coding agent, plus a one-line check for success. Keep every prompt under 90 words. Use Python and Snowflake naming conventions, and if any name I say sounds garbled, use the most sensible name. Use capital placeholders for repo URL, account, user, warehouse, two source schemas written DB1 dot SCHEMA1 and DB2 dot SCHEMA2, and an existing dev schema for the Streamlit app written EXISTING DB dot EXISTING SCHEMA.

The project is a duplicate-table detector for Snowflake, built entirely by Cortex Code inside a GitHub repo. Important constraints: it creates no databases, schemas, tables or procedures and persists nothing; all logic is Python in src slash dupfinder using Snowpark; every stage returns a pandas DataFrame; SQL uses parameter binding; sources are read-only; AI complete only ever receives table names, column names and statistics, never row values; a max rows setting defaulting to twenty million skips bigger tables; commit after each step.

Steps in order. One: clone the repo and create a branch. Two: install Snowflake CLI with pip, create a connection named dev with browser login, test it. Three: write a rules file with the constraints above, create the folders and a readme. Four: a profile module reading information schema tables and columns for a database and schema, skipping views, temporary and transient tables, returning table, row count, bytes, last altered, column list and a fingerprint that is the MD5 of sorted column names and types, plus a script that runs it for both schemas and prints tables sharing a fingerprint across schemas. Five: a keys module that, for tables sharing a fingerprint or seventy percent of columns with another, queries approx count distinct and null count per column, using a ten percent sample above five million rows, picks the column with highest uniqueness and zero nulls, and when two or more exceed point nine nine calls AI complete at temperature zero with names, types and ratios asking for JSON with key and reason. Six: a sketch module that sketches each candidate table once with minhash of the key and minhash of hash star plus distinct key count, keeps the states as JSON in Python, compares each pair across different schemas by passing both states into one query with parse JSON and approximate similarity, computes containment both ways as intersection equals J times A plus B divided by one plus J, and assigns verdicts: exact copy above point nine five row similarity, subset when key containment is above point nine one way only, drifted when above point nine both ways and row similarity below point eight, structure only below point one, otherwise partial overlap. Seven: a report module with a per-column match percentage join for drifted pairs and one AI complete call at temperature point three producing a short steward report grouped by verdict with two sentences per pair, plus a pipeline module with a run function chaining all stages and returning the DataFrames and report text, run end to end from the script. Eight: a Streamlit in Snowflake app using get active session with inputs for the two schemas, a run button calling the pipeline, results kept in session state, and tabs for report, pairs filtered by verdict, keys and drift detail, with snowflake yml and environment yml, deployed with snow streamlit deploy to the existing dev schema. Nine: update the readme, commit, push and open a pull request.